.docname {Telemetry}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Reading | One value from one sensor at one moment | 24.5 °C in room 2 |
    | Tick | Everything one building reports at one moment | 40 readings in one batch |
    | Metric | A kind of reading | temperature, peopleCount |
    | Plugin | One metric's own rules, in one place | The temperature plugin |
    | Microkernel | A small core that knows no metric, plus plugins that add them | Like a phone and its apps |
    | Limit | The value a metric must stay within | Over 28 °C is a breach |
    | Collector | A small box in the building that reads its Wi-Fi routers | One per site |
    | HMAC | A signature that proves the sender holds the building's key | Sent with every batch |

Realises the [Telemetry Ingestion](../domain-model/domain-ingestion.qd) domain. Rust, PostgreSQL with TimescaleDB. Code-level page: [Telemetry Service](../services/telemetry.qd).

The door for every sensor reading. It checks, stores and hands on, and tells when a limit is broken.

---

## Who talks to it

.mermaid
    flowchart LR
        S["Sensors, collector"] -- "signed batches" --> T["telemetry"]
        C["client"] -- "history, limits" --> T
        T -. "every tick" .-> D["dashboard"]
        T == "breaches" ==> N["notification"]
        T -- "commands" --> Dev["Devices"]

Thin: a request. Thick: Kafka. Dotted: Redis.

With `digital-twin` it trades in both directions:

| Direction | What | How |
|---|---|---|
| From `digital-twin` | New building and its rooms | Kafka |
| From `digital-twin` | Room counts from Wi-Fi | Kafka |
| From `digital-twin` | Room limits after an edit | Request |
| To `digital-twin` | "Registration done" | Kafka |
| To `digital-twin` | Wi-Fi ticks | Kafka |
| To `digital-twin` | "Who owns this building?" | Request |

---

## Two directions of change

Two things change often, and for different reasons. Each has its own pattern.

| What changes | Example | Pattern |
|---|---|---|
| Kinds of metric | A new CO₂ sensor | Microkernel: one new plugin |
| Technology around it | Another database or broker | Ports and adapters: one new adapter |

.mermaid
    flowchart LR
        subgraph In["Driving adapters"]
            H["HTTP"]
            K["Kafka in"]
        end
        subgraph Kernel["Kernel: knows no metric"]
            UC["Use cases"]
            REG["Plugin registry"]
            P["Ports"]
        end
        subgraph Plugins
            T1["temperature"]
            T2["peopleCount"]
            T3["airQuality"]
            T4["deviceCount"]
        end
        subgraph Out["Driven adapters"]
            PG["PostgreSQL"]
            R["Redis"]
            KP["Kafka out"]
            TW["digital-twin lookup"]
            DV["Device commands"]
        end
        H --> UC
        K --> UC
        UC --> REG
        REG --> T1 & T2 & T3 & T4
        UC --> P
        P -.-> PG & R & KP & TW & DV

- **The kernel never names a metric.** It asks the registry for the right plugin. A new metric is one new plugin plus one line to register it.
- **A plugin declares everything about its metric:** fields, unit, limits, and the commands its devices accept.
- **The catalog and the check read the same declaration.** What `telemetry` says it accepts is exactly what it accepts.
- A test fails the build if the kernel imports a plugin or a technology, or a plugin imports another plugin.

---

## A batch comes in

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        participant S as Sensor gateway
        participant T as telemetry
        participant DB as PostgreSQL
        participant R as Redis
        participant K as Kafka
        S->>T: one batch, signed
        Note over T: signature, then every reading
        T->>DB: store
        T-->>S: 202
        T->>R: the tick, on telemetry:raw
        Note over T: any value over its limit?
        T->>K: one message per breach

| Rule | Why |
|---|---|
| One bad reading refuses the whole batch | The sender knows exactly what was kept: all or nothing |
| At most 500 readings | One batch cannot hold the service up |
| Store before publishing | Nobody sees a reading that cannot be queried yet |
| Publishing never fails the batch | The reading is already safe; a broker hiccup is only logged |
| Breaches are queued, not awaited | A Kafka outage cannot slow down sensors |

**Example.** A batch holds 40 readings. Reading 17 has no room. The batch is refused, and none of the 40 is stored.

---

## Limits

- A limit is set for a building, and may be set for one room. The room's limit wins.
- Registering a building sets every room's limits in one request, all checked before any is written.
- `deviceCount` never raises a breach. A room full of laptops is not a crowd, and the Wi-Fi estimate is a guess. Crowding stays with `peopleCount`.

**Example.** The building limit for temperature is 28 °C. Room 2, a server room, has its own: 24 °C. A reading of 25 °C breaches in room 2 only.

---

## Wi-Fi ticks

1. The collector sends a signed tick: which router hears which phone.
2. `telemetry` refuses unknown fields, so a raw phone address cannot get in.
3. It counts phones per router room, each phone once, at its loudest router.
4. It hands the tick on to `digital-twin`, which estimates positions.
5. `digital-twin` sends room counts back, stored as readings like any other.

---

## Choices

| Choice | Why |
|---|---|
| One plugin per metric | A new sensor kind touches one place |
| A port for every technology | The kernel is tested with fakes, no database |
| Ask `digital-twin` who owns a building | No copy of someone else's data to keep in step; a failed lookup denies |
| Device commands in its own words | Device URLs and fields live in one adapter only. See [Sensor Actions](../design/sensor-actions.qd) |

Storage and retention: [Telemetry Storage](../design/telemetry-storage.qd).

---

## Gaps

- **Code vs domain: one alert per breaching reading.** The domain says one breach, one alert. The code sends a message per reading, and `notification` mutes repeats for 5 minutes, so a long breach alerts every 5 minutes.
- **Code vs domain: no "Breach Ended".** Nothing is sent when a value comes back within its limit.
